#### Variation 1: Polling with Long-Polling Optimization

**Description**: Standard polling can be inefficient if client polls too frequently or operations complete quickly. Long-polling holds the HTTP connection open until status changes or timeout, reducing unnecessary requests.

**Implementation**:
```csharp
[HttpGet("operations/{id}")]
public async Task<IActionResult> GetOperationStatus(string id,
    [FromQuery] int waitSeconds = 0)
{
    var operation = await _statusStore.GetAsync(id);

    if (operation == null)
        return NotFound();

    // If operation already completed, return immediately
    if (operation.Status == "Completed" || operation.Status == "Failed")
        return Ok(operation);

    // Long-polling: wait for status change or timeout
    if (waitSeconds > 0)
    {
        var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow.AddSeconds(Math.Min(waitSeconds, 30));

        while (DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
        {
            operation = await _statusStore.GetAsync(id);

            if (operation.Status != "Running" && operation.Status != "Accepted")
                return Ok(operation);

            await Task.Delay(1000); // Check every second
        }
    }

    return Ok(operation);
}
```

**When to Use**: Internal APIs where client and server in same datacenter, reducing polling traffic overhead

**Trade-offs**: Holds connections longer (back to original problem at smaller scale); max wait time should be limited to 30-60 seconds

