#### Variation 2: SignalR/WebSocket Push Notifications

**Description**: Instead of polling or callbacks, use WebSocket connection to push real-time status updates to connected clients.

**Implementation**:
```csharp
// SignalR Hub
public class OperationHub : Hub
{
    public async Task SubscribeToOperation(string operationId)
    {
        await Groups.AddToGroupAsync(Context.ConnectionId, operationId);
    }
}

// Background worker pushes updates
public async Task UpdateOperationStatus(string operationId, string status, int progress)
{
    await _statusStore.UpdateAsync(operationId, status, progress);

    // Push real-time update to subscribed clients
    await _hubContext.Clients.Group(operationId).SendAsync("OperationStatusChanged",
        new { operationId, status, progress });
}
```

**Client Code** (JavaScript):
```javascript
const connection = new signalR.HubConnectionBuilder()
    .withUrl("/hubs/operations")
    .build();

connection.on("OperationStatusChanged", (update) => {
    console.log(`Operation ${update.operationId}: ${update.status} (${update.progress}%)`);
    if (update.status === "Completed") {
        fetchResult(update.operationId);
    }
});

connection.start().then(() => {
    connection.invoke("SubscribeToOperation", operationId);
});
```

**When to Use**: Real-time dashboards, administrative interfaces requiring live progress updates, scenarios where polling overhead unacceptable

**Trade-offs**: WebSocket infrastructure required; connection management complexity; may not work through all firewalls/proxies

