#### Variation 4: Inline Result for Fast Operations (Optimistic Pattern)

**Description**: Hybrid approach that returns result inline if operation completes quickly (< 2 seconds), otherwise falls back to async pattern.

**Implementation**:
```csharp
[HttpPost("operations")]
public async Task<IActionResult> SubmitOperation(OperationRequest request)
{
    var operationId = Guid.NewGuid().ToString();

    // Try fast path with timeout
    using (var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
    {
        try
        {
            var result = await _operationService.ExecuteAsync(request, cts.Token);

            // Fast path: operation completed within 2 seconds
            return Ok(result);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Timeout: fall back to async pattern
        }
    }

    // Slow path: use async pattern
    await _statusStore.CreateAsync(operationId, "Accepted");
    await _queue.EnqueueAsync(operationId, request);

    return Accepted(new
    {
        OperationId = operationId,
        StatusUrl = $"/api/operations/{operationId}"
    });
}
```

**When to Use**: Operations with variable execution time (some fast, some slow); mixed workload where most operations complete quickly

**Trade-offs**: Unpredictable client response times; client must handle both synchronous (200 OK with result) and asynchronous (202 Accepted with operation ID) responses

